#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147168 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0f80f6-920a-5eb0-919c-62c8ba67a2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Multiplication and Division as Fraction Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "ba1bbec5-0ff2-52ec-ac2e-876a8ea4c70e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13755" :question/problem "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n![](images/5b/5bc4e442148d5f0b64a16d9236ade1c1c9e962d1b922cc5e21d6cb7073eef45c.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is\n\n$$\n(15\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}) \\times (4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}).\n$$\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}$:\n\n$$\n(15\\, {\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}) \\times (4\\, {\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}})\n$$\n\nWe can now interpret the divisions as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15} 5 \\times \\dfrac 4 {13}\n$$\n\nSo the missing fraction is $\\dfrac{15} 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147169 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2d79438-2593-55c1-a937-c40f8fc6db08" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression as an Equivalent Product of Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "ebdd2229-6712-561c-821b-7286460a6824" :question/math-academy-id "e-13758" :question/problem "Find an expression of the form ![](images/5d/5d571b53dea77754a9eccf1c33b3ca4224f8db534141773f385b3f5ae42a26b2.svg) equivalent to $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\times\\dfrac{9}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n(4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 2}}).\n$$\n\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}$:\n\n$$\n(4\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}})\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the $4\\div 2$ and $9\\div 3$ as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{2} \\times \\dfrac{9}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"}] :question/id #uuid "830169cc-f331-5a91-81d6-374aad29e951" :question/math-academy-id "e-13761" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac{12}{7}\\times \\dfrac{21}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{12}{4}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{21}{7}}_{⏟}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147186 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "36b559ef-d696-580d-813f-c80fc9bc36b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14704" :question/problem "What is ![](images/ef/efa6dc23b03222213c112d946567ac1ee879bbfa007e1245f9ceedbf4f8c0dea.svg) expressed as a fraction in lowest terms?" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n![](images/88/8889a8caf91247de656b139eac0ac1cda307a95304fa3f31c6b66b3b43b4c7ac.svg)\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator (![](images/b9/b992f271508479b55a84d515f24ad943e6d6cebee3cf8ca8eba23c53d2ffce30.svg)) and the second fraction's numerator (![](images/e6/e6c919a9391008f140a302a91592baadf4fda7df09436f355d97029a17a0452a.svg)) have a common \nfactor of ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n![](images/01/01248649d0990953974a79f89b48a1cc95c30f9a5d0614b31c38bacefec9f639.svg)\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) \n\n![](images/15/15c1044aca3df2ff8f02df2e860fefb8d7f640f9bfa333b16bb44d4689aa7361.svg)\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n![](images/7b/7bb4e93fe78704795ace2ead756ff9fc49da5fd9ec1479f2b44e823e37e1be6f.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0f80f6-920a-5eb0-919c-62c8ba67a2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Multiplication and Division as Fraction Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04459a90-5778-53c3-81a4-feacfd08716f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430556} {:answer/id #uuid "38be066b-1060-556f-b0f3-3b2e5b8a2241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3bd8d7-a532-536f-b660-f76e9b30185c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430591} {:answer/id #uuid "c5be527c-e0e3-5fff-9dfe-951de67b43cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/v