-solution "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n(11\\,÷\\,9)\\times (3\\,÷\\,2).\n$$\n\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,9$ and the $÷\\,2:$\n\n$$\n(11\\,÷\\,2)\\times (3\\,÷\\,9)\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the $11÷2$ and $3÷9$ as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0f80f6-920a-5eb0-919c-62c8ba67a2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Multiplication and Division as Fraction Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9904346a-817e-537a-8ee8-129e9a19e00f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9904346a-817e-537a-8ee8-129e9a19e00f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430601} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2eaf7c2-3959-5af5-8438-2a3b96ccd335" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe88fd4-c2df-5ce1-afe8-3a63fe72cba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-195480" :question/problem "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n$(4÷3)\\times (6÷2)=\\frac{4}{2}\\,\\times $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is\n\n$$\n(4\\,÷\\,3)\\times (6\\,÷\\,2).\n$$\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,3$ and the $÷\\,2$:\n\n$$\n(4\\,÷\\,2)\\times (6\\,÷\\,3)\n$$\n\nWe can now interpret the divisions as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{2}\\times \\frac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nSo the missing factor is $\\frac{6}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2d79438-2593-55c1-a937-c40f8fc6db08" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression as an Equivalent Product of Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb0f93d-7294-5dfb-9e3e-41eb24d7f7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb0f93d-7294-5dfb-9e3e-41eb24d7f7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430606} :answer-field/id #uuid "c54d245e-17a7-5113-88c7-9a4ce20205e5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a80469df-5371-5771-a53c-6877f0cc2ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-195599" :question/problem "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n$\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{10}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times \\,\\frac{9}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{10}=(5÷4)\\times (9÷10).\n$$\n\nThen, our expression is\n\n$$\n(5\\,÷\\,4)\\times (9\\,÷\\,10).\n$$\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,4$ and the $÷\\,10$:\n\n$$\n(5\\,÷\\,10)\\times (9\\,÷\\,4)\n$$\n\nWe can now interpret the divisions as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{10}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the missing factor is $\\frac{5}{10}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f71153-9c75-5ef3-91b5-ab96482402c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f71153-9c75-5ef3-91b5-ab96482402c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430604} :answer-field/id #uuid "6098630e-ecf0-5f12-842f-1d3c910f1e8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4879e2d6-8cad-5506-bb76-0080ddea786f" :question/math-academy-id "q-195609" :question/problem "Expressed as an integer, $\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,4)\\times (8\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,4$ and $÷\\,3$:\n \n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,3)\\times (8\\,÷\\,4)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{4}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{9}{3}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{8}{4}}_{⏟}}_{2}=3\\times 2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3}=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bff0f413-a5b9-5bdb-a906-8c4ce718f4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bff0f413-a5b9-5bdb-a906-8c4ce718f4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430613} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d2e9ad-b4e5-5278-b6a7-178a56d479ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36cbce10-7136-5dc9-8dd6-df6f370a71a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-195616" :question/problem "Expressed as an integer, $\\frac{35}{6}\\times \\frac{18}{7}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,6)\\times (18\\,÷\\,7).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,6$ and $÷\\,7$:\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,7)\\times (18\\,÷\\,6)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{7}\\times \\frac{18}{6}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{35}{7}}_{⏟}}_{5}\\times {{\\frac{18}{6}}_{⏟}}_{3}=5\\times 3=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{35}{6}\\times \\frac{18}{7}=15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ed7987-15fc-5e02-8aef-70ab495d4217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430570} {:answer/id #uuid "4f25c2b5-5dd6-57dc-b64b-4bf8d35775e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430576} {:answer/id #uuid "d5772281-7ba0-54de-946f-4873bf9f34f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430619} {:answer/id #uuid "eb730c66-f1b1-5979-8625-159f6e1cb683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430627} {:answer/id #uuid "edbb5a1e-74d7-5142-9edf-c7e568e18402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ed7987-15fc-5e02-8aef-70ab495d4217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430570} :answer-field/id #uuid "53e694dc-c905-5384-9316-1bcd67393757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "826ca900-49ec-524f-b02a-6573662e88a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-208001" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{10}\\times \\frac{6}{7}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{10}\\times \\frac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($10$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6}{10}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6÷2}{10÷2} & =\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{1\\times 3}{7\\times 5}=\\frac{3}{35}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07282d48-88bb-5986-812b-b1ad5940eec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430558} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd3ba50-6268-549b-91c3-d5ecd4bf40ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430559} {:answer/id #uuid "6876307f-3fe0-52d1-8f06-0a368e30f549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430585} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0e8364-97f3-5e5d-8fd9-d2d303e76fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430594} {:answer/id #uuid "e8aad9a7-6637-5001-b622-f519b4527c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07282d48-88bb-5986-812b-b1ad5940eec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430558} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4621eea-9ed0-562c-b850-b7b7a1bd0a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4761ec95-7832-59a1-a543-1121304509d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-208003" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{9}\\times \\frac{12}{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{9}\\times \\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($9$) and the second fraction's numerator ($12$) have a common \nfactor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}\\times \\frac{12}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}\\times \\frac{12÷3}{9÷3} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{1\\times 4}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{4}{15}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb0afc6-4da0-5c91-9872-0603b7132149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430562} {:answer/id #uuid "a97b1730-dd27-5fd8-9002-de4f8ab1da7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430605} {:answer/id #uuid "af632068-8f59-5319-a50f-667d0b0aaee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430608} {:answer/id #uuid "bc05a1f4-a693-5791-b52c-5866bafdf327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430612} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bf1dc5-2c3b-5138-9433-985d40f21e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5bf1dc5-2c3b-5138-9433-985d40f21e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430629} :answer-field/id #uuid "194d737a-ce02-5375-9f0b-24ddf953c2a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b8ecff1-169c-50b4-9995-e59b8594fed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-208021" :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($2$) and the second fraction's denominator ($4$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4}\\times \\frac{5}{7}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the first fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2÷2}{4÷2}\\times \\frac{5}{7} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{5}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{5}{7}=\\frac{1\\times 5}{2\\times 7}=\\frac{5}{14}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001b2fc4-e19b-55f3-88e4-2e4cc199be37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{22}" :